  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is helping the Philippines strengthen its health system through Project BRAVE, and  improving the government&#x2019;s mental health and psychosocial support (MHPSS) services to help Filipinos cope with the impacts of the pandemic.
&#x200d;
Through Project BRAVE, Australia supported the development of evidence-based policies for improving reporting, referral, and response systems; led awareness campaigns and behavioural change interventions (including reaching 99,864 individuals with MHPSS advocacy); developed crisis hotlines, online and remote resources for mental healthcare, suicide prevention and gender-based violence (GBV); and conducted training to equip teachers, education officials and community-level stakeholders on MHPSS promotion, child and GBV protection.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social protection measures</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is supporting the Ministry of Social Services and Development (MSSD) in the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao improve its shock-responsive social protection systems. Australia supported distribution of emergency cash transfers to 4,325 households (25,517 individuals) affected by Typhoon Nalgae. Using UNICEF&#x2019;s social protection systems, diversified cash payment options (digital payments, remittance centres, mobile wallets, commercial banks) were introduced to demonstrate efficient modalities of cash distribution, especially during shocks and emergencies. This is informing MSSD&#x2019;s own social protection systems, including re-designing cash assistance programs and influencing broader shift in policy dialogues on shock responsive social protection in the region.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through Project BRAVE , Australia is helping the Philippines strengthen its health system and improve mental health and psychosocial support services to help Filipinos cope with the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic. Through this program Australia supported: improved reporting, referral and response systems; implementation of guidelines on telehealth and social media peer support (reaching 17,636 individuals); sustainment of crisis hotlines; development of online and remote resources for mental healthcare, suicide prevention and gender-based violence; and training to equip teachers, education officials and community-level stakeholders on mental health and psychosocial support and child and gender-based violence protection.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through Project BRAVE, Australia supports the Philippine Government to strengthen health and protection systems on GBV and child protection. This includes evidence generation, awareness raising, establishing referral systems and online resources, and working in select provinces to establish protection units and provide direct support to survivors.
&#x200d;
Australia supported the following services:
&#x200d;
1. advanced online and face-to-face psychosocial support for 124 GBV survivors and individuals with severe MHPSS needs;
&#x200d;
2. Cash for Protection to 195 GBV survivors to support their recovery and access to essential services.
&#x200d;
3. Cash for Work to facilitators of online support and referrals for 1,940 GBV survivors.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Strengthening Participation and Accountability through CSOs and Subnational and National Government (SPACES) project, Australia facilitated collaboration between nine local governments (LGUs) and 81 civil society organisations (CSOs) to develop action plans that will improve access to affordable and nutritious food. Through the Shock Responsive Social Protection project in the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao (BARMM), Australia also supported the Ministry of Social Services and Development to provide financial assistance to vulnerable communities more efficiently. The pilot of the Financial Assistance System Transformation (FAST) Project introduced beneficiaries to digital payment options through financial service providers, resulting in 800 beneficiaries enrolling for transaction accounts.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
